{:edb/db-before-t 411 :edb/db-after-t 412 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533724 50 #inst "2026-10-04T19:23:50.390Z" 13194139533724 true] [17592186129076 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248587 13194139533724 true] [17592186129076 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248601 13194139533724 true] [17592186129078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248594 13194139533724 true] [17592186129081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248608 13194139533724 true] [17592186248587 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248588 13194139533724 true] [17592186248587 1089 "q-112434" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248587 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533724 true] [17592186248587 1100 "Given that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=7{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+x-1,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248587 1113 #uuid "e713c421-2b00-5045-b24a-0f7fbd12429c" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248587 1138 false 13194139533724 true] [17592186248587 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 7 & -7 & 1 & -1 \\\\  &  & 7 & 0 & 1 \\\\  & 7 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-1}=7{x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)(7{x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $7{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0{)}^{2}-4(7)(1)=-28<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7(0{)}^{3}-7(0{)}^{2}+0-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-1),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-112434-a-1.png)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248589 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248590 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248591 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248592 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248593 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1041 "selection" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248590 13194139533724 true] [17592186248588 1110 #uuid "832ab073-474e-5a8a-b192-13bbc68d27bc" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248589 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-112434-a-2.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248589 1111 #uuid "02938ab2-e9d4-5114-ba4c-ffa258888bd5" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248590 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-112434-a-1.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248590 1111 #uuid "f83af464-0975-5b00-8f5f-59acedb797e4" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248591 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-112434-a-4.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248591 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248591 1111 #uuid "7620add5-7ba1-551b-8e7e-cc0c68b4e524" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248592 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-112434-a-5.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248592 1111 #uuid "e783294d-0c27-51af-ac9a-f7e5994c027c" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248593 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-112434-a-3.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248593 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248593 1111 #uuid "1bbe5672-d6e0-5b4b-91c4-3ce887ce31f8" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248594 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248595 13194139533724 true] [17592186248594 1089 "q-28197" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248594 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533724 true] [17592186248594 1100 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(4-x)({x}^{2}+6)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248594 1113 #uuid "24fcd9b1-0c96-5914-b19d-38120c8b01d7" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248594 1138 false 13194139533724 true] [17592186248594 1145 "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+6=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(6)=-24<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(4-0)({0}^{2}+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(6)}{8} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-e-0.png)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248596 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248597 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248598 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248599 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248600 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1041 "selection" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248598 13194139533724 true] [17592186248595 1110 #uuid "4717dd12-e65c-550b-8dc4-1967aa72efff" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248596 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-5.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248596 1111 #uuid "0f852022-e1f7-5543-971f-4e1505078600" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248597 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-3.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248597 1111 #uuid "7bf54fe6-22f2-5727-b96a-a2828da7f313" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248598 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-1.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248598 1111 #uuid "a0c45b64-5090-5a18-9e7d-13f38da33b10" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248599 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-4.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248599 1111 #uuid "b832742c-4a0b-514d-a4b4-44d15e07c08f" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248600 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-2.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248600 1111 #uuid "a4cdeb7f-ecec-5f1b-bea4-6e92b69b31ec" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248601 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248602 13194139533724 true] [17592186248601 1089 "q-39032" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248601 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533724 true] [17592186248601 1100 "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5x-6,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248601 1113 #uuid "438b67c5-d731-547c-860c-c6e1368047db" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248601 1138 false 13194139533724 true] [17592186248601 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 5 & -6 \\\\  &  & 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\  & 1 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-2}={x}^{2}-x+3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-2)({x}^{2}-x+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}-x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-11<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}-3(0{)}^{2}+5(0)-6=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-6),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-1.png)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248603 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248604 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248605 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248606 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248607 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1041 "selection" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248607 13194139533724 true] [17592186248602 1110 #uuid "e67a7594-2bd2-5bc7-bb9c-21f2a461d7a8" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248603 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-2.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248603 1111 #uuid "966f35c2-66c5-5091-abf5-0be88c14a7cd" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248604 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-3.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248604 1111 #uuid "7a813be0-03f1-510f-9e94-caacd47988a2" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248605 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-5.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248605 1111 #uuid "8a13d929-24ae-58fb-bacb-94cc50f1ff7d" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248606 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-4.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248606 1111 #uuid "a2ac82d8-dc15-51eb-a2ff-45ed87b9ab28" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248607 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-1.png" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533724 true] [17592186248607 1111 #uuid "4deab048-da77-52b3-a408-72371f192a81" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248608 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248609 13194139533724 true] [17592186248608 1089 "q-39091" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248608 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533724 true] [17592186248608 1100 "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248608 1113 #uuid "752afc64-d791-502e-80b3-671a34272a42" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248608 1138 false 13194139533724 true] [17592186248608 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\  &  & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\  & -1 & -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-2} & =-{x}^{2}-x-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+x+2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-2)({x}^{2}+x+2).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $({x}^{2}+x+2)=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={1}^{2}-4(1)(2)=-7<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-(0{)}^{3}+(0{)}^{2}+4=4\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39091-a-1.png)" 13194139533724 true] [17592186248609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248610 13194139533724 true] [17592186248609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248611 13194139533724 true] [17592186248609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248612 13194139533724 true] [17592186248609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248613 13194139533724 true] [17592186248609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248614 1319